    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm studying for my diesel certs and I'm a bit stuck on turbochargers. Can you explain the basic cycle of how the exhaust gases actually turn into increased intake manifold pressure? Just need the mechanical steps.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Turbochargers use the energy from hot, high-velocity exhaust gases to spin a turbine wheel. This spinning turbine is mechanically linked to a compressor wheel, which is what does the real work. The compressor draws in ambient air, compresses it, and then pushes this pressurized, denser air into the engine's intake manifold. This forced induction is what increases the intake manifold pressure, allowing the engine to burn more fuel efficiently.",
      "source": "target",
