    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I've noticed that when they're working hard, there's this really distinct high-pitched whistling or whining sound coming from the unit. Does that sound have something to do with the actual physics of the air moving through the turbine, or is it just friction?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That high-pitched whine you hear is usually a combination of both, but it's often related to the aerodynamics of the airflow. When the turbine is spinning at high RPMs, the air rushing past the compressor blades and through the plumbing creates significant turbulence and high-velocity shear forces. Think of it like a very fast, very noisy stream of air, which is a sound effect of the intense pressure differential the turbo is creating. It's not just simple friction, but the sound of high-speed compressible fluid dynamics.",
      "source": "target",
